      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While your position involves ethically complex trade-offs, a historian can still argue that colonial powers inadvertently supplied the foundational state-building structures—like unified legal codes, standardized currency, and rail networks—that pre-nationalist movements in Africa and South Asia lacked. Proponents claim these institutions created a shared infrastructure that allowed post-colonial nations to function as modern states, facilitating trade and administration better than the fragmented traditional systems previously existed. The counter-argument to position against would not be the morality of colonization, but rather whether these specific administrative legacies were neutral tools for development or mere mechanisms of exploitation that inevitably fell into the hands of corrupt or inefficient post-independence regimes, making them insufficient to drive genuine prosperity.",
